   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "## scene surroundings \u00b7 the street around the building\n\n**You need:** Blender 4.x installed (it runs in the background). No AI models.\n\n**Do:** Queue. The sample building stands on a sample square: neighbours in its row, a row across the square, a block with a courtyard. The plan shows the fit from above, the previz shows the clay model as the picture on the building, seen from three spots on the square.\n\n**Your own place:** open a map, right click on the middle of your facade, copy the coordinates and paste them into `location` of **scene surroundings** (`latitude, longitude`). The buildings around come from OpenStreetMap. Downloads are off until you allow them: write `surroundings_download = on` under `[settings]` in `kubakub.ini` (pack folder) and restart ComfyUI. Then it is one download per place (the node sends the coordinates and the radius to overpass-api.de), and after that it works offline. The node finds which way your facade faces and leaves the building's own footprint out.\n\n**Your own picture on the building:** connect it to `matrix` of **scene preview**. It must be made in the projector's view, the same frame as the `clay` picture of scene render. A festival file with its own projection camera: unplug the **projector** node and set `width` / `height` of scene render to 0, then the file's camera and matrix size are used and a picture made on the festival template fits. Too bright: lower `gain`; `ambient` is the light on everything the projector does not reach.\n\n**If the fit is off:** the advanced inputs `turn_deg`, `along_m`, `out_m` move the street against the model; `facing_deg` sets the facade's compass direction by hand. Watch the plan.\n\n**Your own surroundings:** a city model or a Houdini export in the same space as the building goes into `file`; then nothing is downloaded.\n\n**Light it:** **scene relight** renders the building in its street with lamps and an HDRI, your picture cast on it from the projector (`matrix`; here the clay stands in for it). Lamps: one per line in `lights`, or by hand on the plan the node shows after its first run: drag a lamp, turn the mouse wheel over it for its height, click it for watts, colour and size; the `+` buttons add lamps. `environment` picks the HDRI (night, sunset, city ... or your own file).\n\n**Where the camera stands:** drag the violet ring on the plan (`camera = audience`) and the dot in front of it to turn it; switch `live` on and every drop renders again (lower `resolution_scale` and `samples` for speed). Or set `camera = previz` to take the very viewpoint of **scene preview** (its first spot; `previz_spot` picks another). `camera = projector` is the matrix view of the building alone.\n\n**Too bright:** lower `exposure`, the lamps' watts or `projector_brightness` (1 = as in the previz). A projection only shows where the lamps leave the wall dark.\n\n**Looks:** on **scene preview**, `clay_color` picks the clay's colour and `shader` switches between clay, wireframe and clay + wireframe.\n\n**Then try:** connect the surroundings' `scene` to **scene walkthrough**, or to **pieces render** with `view = audience`; `view = street` on the surroundings node.\n"
   ],
   "title": "read me"
  },
